            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

This older 1971 house is reasonably air tight, however does have some key air leakage locations that should be addressed:



** Air leakage associated with the older aluminium windows &amp; patio door



** Around the attic access panel (can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



** Around the old wood stove chimney in the basement



** An intentional hole is the 6" combustion air supply pipe in the mechanical room



** Some leakage through the header space between the basement &amp; main floor (that we looked at together with my Infrared Camera)



Savings: In conjunction with your planned upgrades for this house, you should achieve at least a 10% reduction in natural air leakage, that could potentially save $ 109 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 5.4 GJ, and qualify for a $ 500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). Achieving 20% qualifies for $750, and achieving a 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior main floor walls of your house are 2x4 (R12) and I have modeled the addition of another R20 (eg. 4" of extruded polystyrene rigid foam board) insulation to the exterior of the main floor walls. In order to upgrade 100% of the main floor exterior walls, you will need to add R20 to the wall that adjoins the garage.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R20, you could potentially save $ 519 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 28.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 8,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



NOTE: The cost &amp; energy savings outlined above include the savings from adding R20 to the outside of the rear walkout foundation wood walls and the perimeter of the header space between the basement &amp; main floor (when you insulate the upper main floor walls the insulation &amp; new siding will automatically extend down over the basement header space). There is an additional rebate for adding this R20 to the rear basement walkout (wood walls section) and the basement header - see the next section of this report.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <foundation>

Your basement is mostly concrete walls with interior framing, insulation &amp; drywall. There is a section along the back and back-left corner that are 2x6 wood framed (represent about 28% of total basement wall area). If you are going to add R20 to the main floor walls above, you will also add R20 to the exterior of these wood framed wall sections. Also, the exterior insulation &amp; new siding of the main floor walls will extend down over the full exterior perimeter of the basement header space.



Savings: By adding R20 of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the exterior of the basement wood framed walls &amp; the full perimeter header space between the basement &amp; main floor could potentially qualify for up to an $ 850 rebate ($350 for basement &amp; $500 for the header area) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade. I have included the estimated annual cost and energy savings with the previous Main Floor Walls upgrade recommendation.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</foundation>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house (10 includes main floor + basement and the aluminum patio door) are eligible to be upgraded in conjunction with your planned exterior wall insulation &amp; siding project. A few of them are newer, however you may still choose to upgrade them in conjunction with the new "thicker" walls. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all 8 of the windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing these 10 windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 303 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 12.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ $2,500 in rebates (10 @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <doors />
                <HotWaterSystem>

As you have a combination heating &amp; hot water system, please see my recommendations in the previous section.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is heated with a combination water heater, where your 50 USG GSW Superflue hot water tank is used to provide hot water to a fancoil inside your Lifebreath clean air furnace to circulate throughout the house for heating (calculated heating efficiency of about 80%). This same tank also provides the domestic hot water you use in the house for cleaning, bathing, washing, etc. (at a rated Energy Factor of 0.62 EF). The Lifebreath also serves as a Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) providing stale/fresh air exchanges for the house with about a 63% efficiency.



The above system is controlled by a thermostat on the main level of the house, which means the basement areas receives a portion of the heat, based on the percentage of air flow that is directed down there ........ and where the Lifebreath is drawing its' return air from.



While I was there, I heard you mention the wood pellet stove in the basement is operated pretty much all the time during the heating season to manage heating to the basement &amp; supplement the heating of the main floor (via whatever heat rises up through the open stairway). In fact, you turned it on again as soon as I was done the blower door air leakage test.



I see very few of these combination systems where a standard hot water tank is the source, however they were quite common in southern Ontario, where I evaluated alot of them. As the heating demand in the NWT is about 200% - 300% higher than southern locations of Canada, I usually see a true "Combi Boiler" installation in NWT communities - where the heating source is a super high efficiency "boiler" first, that also provides instantaneous, on-demand domestic hot water also at very high efficiencies. With the flexibility of a "boiler" installation, you can have multiple zones, so the Lifebreath can serve the upper floor via the main floor thermostat and another heating zone (with its' own thermostat) can serve parts or all of the basement. You can then choose to use the pellet stove (for zone heat to the basement) as much or as little as you want.



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? 95% AFUE combi boiler to both heat your house (100% of the basement &amp; main floor) and provide high efficiency (0.87 EF) on-demand domestic hot water. This system will direct vent to the outside and will no longer require the 6" combustion air supply pipe. Also, please ensure your pellet stove has its' own dedicated combustion air pipe (directly connected to the back of the stove) before you eliminate the 6" combustion air pipe in the mechanical room.



Savings:

By upgrading to an ENERGY STAR?????? 95% AFUE combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water, you could potentially save $ 3,254 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 34.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $16,350 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $8,000 for adding R20 to the main floor walls

     - $   850 for adding R20 to the wood-framed section of the basement walls (and full perimeter header)

     - $2,500 for replacing 10 windows with new eligible units

     - $2,500 for an eligible combination (heat &amp; hot water) boiler

     - $   500 for a 10% air leakage reduction

     - $2,000 for the bonus rebate (a 30% - 40% reduction in total gigajoules)



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than the DHERP does not apply - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be for the combination boiler, however this would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - ECM domestic water circulation pump - 50% up to $175

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see next section.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood fan, 3 x bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 8.0 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could compete against the chimney to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current hot water heater and pellet stove are "power vented" units that draw combustion air from inside the home.



You previously had a 6" combustion air supply pipe brought into the mechanical room to provide combustion air for these appliance. If you upgrade to a high efficiency combination boiler &amp; install a dedicated outside combustion air pipe into the back of the pellet boiler, you will no longer need the 6" combustion air pipe.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - A kitchen range hood fan

        - 3 bathroom fans

        - A clothes dryer



You may want toupgrade some of the bathroom exhaust fans with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified units. You may want to consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.33 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.37 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $0.80 per litre 

Propane - $2.46 per m3 for the piped system; or $0.66 per litre delivered 

Wood - $315.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.20 per bag, $268.12 per tonne bulk delivered



